Qualifications to the review

1. A definability or conservativity theorem does not prove that a calculus
   has no contribution. It can have new judgments, useful restrictions,
   observational equivalences, or a new correspondence theorem. In the
   reviewed rewrite, no such original result had been established. That is
   the substantive gap.

2. Neither Nash equilibrium nor SPE is a property of a single execution.
   Nash equilibrium can be studied on an appropriate reduced normal form,
   with its deviation structure. This differs from observing the actual
   trace. The manuscript now gives a same-trace/different-Nash counterexample.

3. Audiences alone cannot imply hiding until a particular opening command.
   An authorized participant can copy c into d and open d first. This
   counterexample applies even to an ideal commitment service.
   The revised theorem therefore has a substantive release condition:
   protected values and their potentially dependent cells remain outside
   the observer's audience throughout the certified prefix.
   The proof allows dependent private cells to have different instantiated
   types in the two runs; only unmarked cells require a common comparison.
   The fixed schedule also excludes timing and refusal channels.

4. The ideal store model shares the fixed-payload/open pattern of
   Canetti--Fischlin's functionality. It is not their entire UC model and
   establishes no cryptographic realization or adversarial composition result.

5. Restoring FM(K), realization, or a stabilizer-based no-go statement would
   require a separate originality audit. The assessment's novelty claims
   are not certificates of novelty. Coalition forceability is also an
   analysis the user explicitly rejected as the central meaning.

Concrete mathematical material retained

Full strategies use A x (Pi x:A. S_i(H)) at an owned choice, retaining
continuations for every alternative. Their reduction to endpoint plans
preserves complete execution. It does not preserve SPE status.

For finite perfect-information one-winner games, full pure SPE profiles
yield the following binary operation on nonempty winner sets:

    S star_r T = {r}       if S={r} or T={r}
                 S union T otherwise.

The manuscript derives this exactly from backward induction, proves the
owned operations associative, commutative, idempotent, and equivariant,
and identifies the generated values: two singletons at K=2; every nonempty
subset at K>=3.

At K>=3 the distributive identity

    (a star_r b) star_s c
      = (a star_s c) star_r (b star_s c)

fails already on the leaves 0,1,2 with r=0,s=1: the two values are {0,2}
and {0,1,2}. Thus there is no injective homomorphism into a product of
Boolean algebras where each owned operation is coordinatewise meet or join.

This is a concrete algebraic target for the intended non-forceability
direction. Its correctness does not establish its originality. The draft
explicitly compares it with backward induction and higher-order games,
and distinguishes it from the monadic selection-function product studied
by Bolt--Hedges--Zahn. Their negative result is not contradicted.

